ngly the economy's  gains have gone to the top the richest  one  tenth of one percent mr chairman as you  said i want to just underscore this  there is just one tenth of one percent  has almost as much wealth  as the bottom 90 put together  and again the compensation packages of  the top executives big companies ceos  have soared from an average of 20 times  that of the typical worker 40 years ago  or  60 times when i was labor secretary to  320 times  today uh and you know the the pandemic  has just made all of this a much more  stark america's 660 billionaires  have together become 1.3 trillion  dollars  richer this would be enough by the way  this 1.3 trillion  that they have gained during the past  year would be enough for them to give  every american  a 3 900 check and still be as rich  as they were before the pandemic the  american rescue plan  just enacted is helpful in this regard  but i think it's very important  for this committee to look at the  underlying structure of power  power is a word that we don't use very  often when we talk about the economy  but there has been a huge shift in power  over the last 40 years  a shift toward very large corporations  and senator graham is absolutely right  there has got to be much more emphasis  on fighting monopolies and it's  monopolies both  in terms of big high-tech companies also  monopolies uh in terms of high finance  uh big pharma i mean you go around this  country today and you see  more concentration more economic  concentration  than we've seen at any time in the last  60 years  and also that economic concentration  translates  into political power we've got a  severely imbalanced political economy  we have fewer than seven percent of our  workers are in unions today 50 years ago  over a third of workers in the private  sector were unionized  50 years ago giant corporations didn't  have the power to suppress  prevailing wages they didn't have  platoons of washington lobbyists  which way which they have to